"""
Catalogue of minimum aberration two-level fractional factorial split-plot designs.

Generated by ``scripts/generate_split_plot_catalogue.py``; do not edit by hand.

``_SPLIT_PLOT_GENERATORS[(k1, k2, n_runs, n_whole_plots)] = (wlp, generators)``
for k1 whole-plot and k2 subplot factors.  With r1 = log2(n_whole_plots) and
r = log2(n_runs), the base factors are the first r1 whole-plot factors (base
factors 0, ..., r1 - 1) and the first r - r1 subplot factors (base factors
r1, ..., r - 1).  The generators define, in order, the remaining k1 - r1
whole-plot factors and then the remaining subplot factors, each as the
product of the listed base factors.  ``wlp`` is the word length pattern
(A3, A4, ..., Ak).  The comment gives the design as k1.k2.p1.p2, the
notation of Bingham and Sitter (1999).

The designs follow the rules of Bingham, D. and Sitter, R. R. (1999),
"Minimum-Aberration Two-Level Fractional Factorial Split-Plot Designs",
Technometrics 41(1), 62-70: whole-plot generators contain whole-plot base
factors only, subplot generators contain at least one subplot base factor.
Each design has the lexicographically smallest word length pattern among all
designs that obey these rules (exhaustive search); ties go to the first
choice of generator columns in Yates order.

Range: 4 runs up to 3 factors, 8 runs up to 7 factors, 16 runs up to 15 factors, 32 runs up to 15 factors.

The word length patterns were checked against B&S Table 1 (8 runs) and
Table 2 (16 runs).  They agree for all designs but one: for 5.7.2.6 B&S print
(16, 39, 48, 48, 48, 39, 16, 0, 0, 1), the pattern of the row above, but their
own design (whole-plot columns 3 5, subplot columns 9-14 of their Table 3) has
(17, 38, 44, 52, 54, 33, 12, 4, 1), and the exhaustive search shows that no
5.7.2.6 design does better.  The designs with no subplot generators (p2 = 0)
have the word length pattern of the minimum aberration fraction of the
whole-plot factors (B&S p. 66).
"""

